@@ -251,6 +251,17 @@ A default priority for all tasks can also be specified using the
 
     app.conf.task_default_priority = 5
 
+With RabbitMQ, higher priority values indicate higher priority: a task with
+priority ``9`` is delivered before a task with priority ``0``. This follows
+the AMQP specification, where higher numbers indicate higher priority.
+
+.. note::
+
+    Priority ordering is broker-specific. RabbitMQ (and other AMQP brokers)
+    treat higher numbers as higher priority, while some other transports
+    (such as Redis, see below) treat lower numbers as higher priority.
+    Always check the documentation for the broker you're using.
+
 .. _amqp-primer:
 
 
@@ -276,6 +287,13 @@ This means that even though there are 10 (0-9) priority levels, these are
 consolidated into 4 levels by default to save resources. This means that a
 queue named celery will really be split into 4 queues.
 
+.. note::
+
+    Unlike RabbitMQ, the Redis transport treats **lower** priority values as
+    **higher** priority: a task with priority ``0`` is processed before a task
+    with priority ``9``. This is the opposite of the AMQP/RabbitMQ convention,
+    so take care when switching brokers.
+
 The highest priority queue will be named celery, and the other queues will
 have a separator (by default `\x06\x16`) and their priority number appended to
 the queue name.
